# THE CANONICAL DIGESTS OF EVERY FILE THE FLEET COPIES.
#
# Everything under fleet/templates/tests/ is COPIED, in full, into every
# repository that adopts the shared release guards. Copies drift, and a
# drifted copy still reports success. The fleet has been bitten by
# exactly this twice: a capability tuple hand-copied at 21 sites in this
# repository that had already drifted before anyone looked, and
# capa_authgate's check_tag_version.sh becoming a second copy whose two
# versions immediately printed different messages.
#
# TWO KINDS OF ENTRY.
#
#   REGION: tests/test_release_wiring.sh and
#   tests/test_wiring_mutations.sh have to say repo-specific things, so
#   each is split by two marker lines into a CONFIG region, which is
#   repo-specific by design, and everything else, which is meant to be
#   byte-identical everywhere. What is recorded is the SHA-256 of
#   everything OUTSIDE the config region, markers included.
#
#   WHOLE: tests/test_shared_regions.sh, tests/test_guard_pins.sh and
#   .github/workflows/checks.yml have no repo-specific content at all,
#   so the whole file is recorded.
#   They carry no CONFIG block on purpose. The config region is the only
#   part of a shared file that nothing digests, so a file that needs no
#   configuration is given none, and with it none of the marker-shape
#   failure modes, none of the grammar and none of the allowlist.
#
# The drift check is itself a whole-file entry, which is what stops its
# own table of checked files from being quietly shortened: doing that
# used to remove a file from the check while every remaining assertion
# still passed.
#
# The KIND of each entry is not recorded in this file. It lives in the
# tables in fleet/shared_region_digest.sh and in the adopter-side check,
# which tests/test_fleet_templates.py holds to each other, so this file
# stays a plain list of `<sha256>  <path>` and an adopter copying
# numbers out of it has one thing to copy.
#
# The paths are the paths an ADOPTER sees. The canonical file is the
# same path under fleet/templates/, which is the mapping the adopter
# check applies to fetch it.
#
# WHO READS THIS:
#
#   * tests/test_fleet_templates.py, offline, which runs the real
#     adopter-side check against these templates and refuses any
#     disagreement with the numbers below;
#   * every adopter's .github/shared-regions.sha256, which copies these
#     numbers and adds a `revision` line naming the commit they were
#     copied from. That revision is INDEPENDENT of the guard pin in
#     .github/guard-pins.sha256, on purpose: one shared revision would
#     make every guard bump force a wiring re-audit and every wiring
#     bump force a guard re-audit, and that friction is the likeliest
#     reason for the whole exercise to be skipped.
#
# TO REGENERATE AFTER A DELIBERATE CHANGE TO A TEMPLATE:
#
#   bash fleet/shared_region_digest.sh
#
# Then bump each adopter's record, revision line included, which is what
# makes the change an adoption rather than a drift.

964679fd80d680d0303f1afb852d12d7845b1cf729d3bf39797249134c52388d  tests/test_release_wiring.sh
fed5e363ed8e42160821e8da5bd4cb37624c4b9f452470f8b582679e878a2fce  tests/test_wiring_mutations.sh
47a9c72c36cb003f0b3288be5c92d629c8a83f48cc1493a2e00342be21d191c4  tests/test_shared_regions.sh
42a9e689ade60a7494d9000d26d1e09518ea01b1dd3646850979abadb7078693  tests/test_guard_pins.sh
f98d7f84e43844f4dcaa76aa92cbfea8c3cce54456819ebbb0a0434ee54e346f  .github/workflows/checks.yml
